# Changelog
All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.0.0/),
and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).

## [6.3.1] - 2026-10-19
### Fixed
- System check `codenerix.E006` no longer imports `codenerix.views`, which reads `STATIC_URL` at import: in 6.3.0, a project without django-haystack that leaves `STATIC_URL` unset (Django's default `None`) had every management command (`check`, `migrate`, `runserver`...) stop with `TypeError: expected str, bytes or os.PathLike object, not NoneType`, even with no codenerix view routed. The check now looks for `GenList` subclasses only when the project's URLconf has loaded `codenerix.views` itself.
- `manage.py migrate_uuids --target 32` removes the dashes before narrowing the columns to `CHAR(32)`. It narrowed first, so on MySQL every dashed UUID was cut to its first 32 characters (lookups then match nothing, with "Migration completed successfully") or, under a strict `sql_mode`, the run failed with error 1265. The `--target 36` refusal on MySQL, which points to `--target 32`, is now safe to follow. `--target 36` is unchanged.

## [6.3.0] - 2026-10-19
### Added
- `OTP_VALID_WINDOW` (default `1`): steps accepted on each side of the current one when `OTPAuth` verifies a 30 s TOTP code, as RFC 6238 recommends. Until now the direct TOTP and the non-mail paths used pyotp's default of `0`, so a correct code typed around the 30 s step boundary was refused; the OTP by email keeps its own window of one 5 minute step. Set it to `0` to keep the old behaviour.
- System check `codenerix.E006`: a view routed by `ROOT_URLCONF` that derives from `GenList` with `haystack = True` while django-haystack is not installed. 6.2.0 made the extra optional, and such a view passed `manage.py check` and answered 500 (`ImproperlyConfigured`) on its first request. The package is probed with `importlib.util.find_spec`, never imported by the check; views that are not routed are not reported.
- System check `codenerix.E007`: a `HAYSTACK_CONNECTIONS` entry whose `ENGINE` starts with `codenerix.contrib.`, removed in 6.2.0. With django-haystack still installed that configuration passes `check` and raises `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'codenerix.contrib'` on the first search, and on every model save with a realtime signal processor. A project that keeps `"haystack"` in `INSTALLED_APPS` after the package is gone cannot be reported by any check: Django stops while populating the apps, before the checks run.

### Fixed
- `manage.py migrate_uuids` refuses with a `CommandError` the target that would break UUID columns on the connected database, before running anything: `--target 36` where Django stores UUIDs as 32 hex characters (MySQL, MariaDB < 10.7: dashed values would never match a lookup) and `--target 32` where it stores dashed values (MariaDB >= 10.7, `has_native_uuid_field`: every write would fail with "Data too long"). It used to accept both on any MySQL-family database.
- `manage.py migrate_uuids` prints, with the plan, the size of every table it would rebuild (`information_schema.TABLES`, rows and data plus index length) and the free space of the server's data directory (`@@datadir`) when that directory is on the host running the command, since `ALTER ... MODIFY` copies each table next to the original. With `--execute` it refuses to start when the free space is below the sum of those sizes, unless `--skip-space-check` is given; in dry-run and when the data directory is on another host it only prints what to check. With MariaDB's `innodb_alter_copy_bulk` `ON` (the 11.x default) the copy also builds the indexes through a merge-sort file in `innodb_tmpdir` (`tmpdir` by default) of about the size of the table being rebuilt, so that directory is checked too, against the largest table, with the same refusal rule; the message gives `SET GLOBAL innodb_alter_copy_bulk=OFF` as the alternative (row-by-row copy, no merge file). On a tmpfs `/tmp` that file fills the RAM and the ALTER aborts after writing gigabytes, leaving the table untouched. New `--disable-copy-bulk`: with `--execute`, runs `SET GLOBAL innodb_alter_copy_bulk=OFF` before the rebuilds and `=ON` afterwards (also when a statement fails), and refuses to start, naming the manual `SET GLOBAL`, when the database user lacks the privilege (`SUPER`); the variable has no session scope, so the change applies to every session of the server meanwhile, and a process killed before the restore leaves it off until it is set by hand or the server restarts. The option sends nothing when the variable is already `OFF` or does not exist (MySQL, MariaDB before 10.11.9), and nothing in dry-run.
- `codenerix.E003` and the debug gate accept a project middleware that derives from both `codenerix.debug_middleware.UpdateCacheMiddleware` and `codenerix.debug_middleware.FetchFromCacheMiddleware` by multiple inheritance: it bypasses debug requests on both halves, but 6.1.0 compared it against `codenerix.debug_middleware.CacheMiddleware` only, reported it and switched the debug tools off. Each half is now checked on its own.

### Documentation
- Since 6.1.0, with a debug tool installed, `manage.py check` and the first request to a debug tool import every entry of `MIDDLEWARE` to classify the page cache middlewares. Django imports them itself when it builds the handler, so the cost is nil, but `check` and `migrate` did not import project middlewares before. An entry that fails to import is treated as not a page cache and left to Django's own error.

## [6.2.0] - 2026-10-15
### Removed (breaking)
- `codenerix.contrib` and its `haystack_engines` module (`AsciifoldingElasticSearchEngine`, `AsciifoldingElasticBackend`). Projects that set `'ENGINE': 'codenerix.contrib.haystack_engines.AsciifoldingElasticSearchEngine'` in `HAYSTACK_CONNECTIONS` get an `ImportError` when Haystack loads its connections: copy the class into the project, or use Haystack's own `haystack.backends.elasticsearch7_backend.Elasticsearch7SearchEngine`.
- `elasticsearch` is no longer a dependency.

### Changed (breaking)
- `django-haystack` is no longer a dependency: it moves to the optional extra `haystack`. Views with `haystack = True` need `pip install django-codenerix[haystack]` (or `django-haystack` in the project's own dependencies) plus the search backend the project uses; without it, `get_queryset()` raises `ImproperlyConfigured` naming the view. Projects that relied on `django-codenerix` pulling in `django-haystack` or `elasticsearch` transitively must now list them themselves: in particular, a project with `"haystack"` in `INSTALLED_APPS` that never listed `django-haystack` gets `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'haystack'` at startup after the upgrade.

## [6.1.0] - 2026-10-14
### Changed (breaking)
- System check `codenerix.E001` is split by kind of problem, so each can be silenced on its own: `codenerix.E001` (removed, malformed or public debug access settings), `codenerix.E002` (a debug tool behind the page cache middlewares), `codenerix.E003` (a page cache that debug requests would not bypass: stock middlewares next to a debug tool, added again after `autoload()`, or a project subclass), `codenerix.E004` (a toolbar `SHOW_TOOLBAR_CALLBACK` that ignores `DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS`) and `codenerix.E005` (more than one middleware of a debug tool). Projects that list `"codenerix.E001"` in `SILENCED_SYSTEM_CHECKS` see the other ids, and those stop `runserver` and `migrate`. Silencing never changed the gates, which still fail closed on every one of them. New `codenerix.debug.debug_access_checks()` returns `(check id, message)` pairs; `debug_access_errors()` still returns the messages alone.
- `autoload(DEBUG_PANEL=True)` (with the toolbar installed) and `autoload(SNIPPET_SCREAM=True)` (with `DEBUG`) raise `ValueError`: django-debug-panel 0.8.3, its last release, imports `django.core.urlresolvers`, removed in Django 2.0, and django-snippetscream 0.0.7 is Python 2 code. Both configurations already stopped Django at startup with an `ImportError`. The keywords are still accepted, so settings that pass `False` keep working. `DebugPanelMiddleware` is no longer treated as a toolbar middleware.
- `autourl(..., SPAGHETTI=True)` puts django_spaghetti's `/plate/` behind the debug gate: it answers 404 unless `codenerix.debug.is_debug_request()` accepts the request (`DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS`, and a superuser login by default), and is sent with `Cache-Control: no-store`. It was open to any client with `DEBUG` on. Route and namespace (`spaghetti:plate`) are unchanged.

### Security
- System check `codenerix.E003` reports a project subclass of Django's page cache middlewares (`UpdateCacheMiddleware`, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware`, `CacheMiddleware`) that does not derive from the `codenerix.debug_middleware` class of the same name, when a debug tool is installed, and the debug tools fail closed on it. `autoload()` can only replace the stock dotted paths: it runs while settings load and cannot import project middlewares. Such a subclass was neither replaced nor reported, so debug requests were served from and stored in the page cache, as in 5.3.3 and earlier. Subclasses must derive from `codenerix.debug_middleware.UpdateCacheMiddleware`, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` or `CacheMiddleware`, which behave as Django's for every other request. The middleware order check also counts such subclasses as page cache middlewares. Projects in this situation that start today get `runserver` and `migrate` refused until the subclass is fixed.
- `codenerix.E005` reports more than one profiler middleware (`pyinstrument.middleware.ProfilerMiddleware` and `codenerix.debug_middleware.ProfilerMiddleware`, or either one listed twice), and more than one toolbar middleware, and the tool concerned fails closed. Two profilers run on the same request: `?profile` answered 500 to the allowed IP, and to every client when `PYINSTRUMENT_SHOW_CALLBACK` was not set. Typical cause: pyinstrument's own middleware added after `autoload()` installed the codenerix one.

### Fixed
- With `DEBUG_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER`, a `SESSION_ENGINE` that cannot be imported no longer answers 500 to allowed IPs carrying a session cookie: the gate fails closed. Without `SessionMiddleware` in `MIDDLEWARE`, nothing else imports the engine.

### Changed
- `codenerix.E003` names every stock page cache middleware it finds next to a debug tool, one message each. 6.0.1 named only the first, so fixing the settings for one left the error on under another name.

### Documentation
- The debug gate reads the superuser from the session cookie only: a superuser authenticated later in the chain, by `codenerix.authbackend.TokenAuthMiddleware` or `OTPAuthMiddleware` setting `request.user`, never sees the debug tools.
- Loading the session in the gate has the side effects `SessionMiddleware` has on the same request: the `file` backend deletes and recreates an expired or undecodable session file, `cached_db` fills the cache on a miss; `db` and `signed_cookies` write nothing (6.0.0 said "without side effects"). A user object without `get_session_auth_hash()` is rejected, where Django's `get_user()` accepts it unverified.
- Correction to 6.0.0, "Toolbar captures are also always cache misses now": not for views decorated with `cache_page`, whose cache middleware instance `autoload()` does not see. Nothing leaks (the view cache stores the response before the toolbar runs), but a repeated capture shows only the cache GET.
- `?profile` on a URL that ends in a slash needs the slash (`/page/?profile`): without it pyinstrument profiles `CommonMiddleware`'s redirect, not the view.
- For an allowed IP carrying a session cookie, the gate loads the session and `SessionMiddleware` loads it again. Kept on purpose: handing the gate's user to `AuthenticationMiddleware` would skip `get_user()`'s session rotation and flush.

### Maintenance
- CI: the `uv run --no-sync --no-build` steps drop `--locked`, which does nothing without a sync; the lock is checked by `uv sync --locked` in each job's sync step.

## [6.0.1] - 2026-10-14
### Changed
- System check `codenerix.E001` names the real cause when a stock page cache middleware (`django.middleware.cache.UpdateCacheMiddleware`, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` or `CacheMiddleware`) is in `MIDDLEWARE` together with the `codenerix.debug_middleware` subclass that `autoload()` put in its place. That happens when settings code runs after `autoload()` and adds the middleware again because it tests `MIDDLEWARE` for the stock path only (`if "django.middleware.cache.UpdateCacheMiddleware" not in MIDDLEWARE: ...`), so the page cache runs twice. The message used to suggest adding the page cache before calling `autoload()`, which those projects already do; it now says the middleware was added again after `autoload()` replaced it and that such checks must also accept the `codenerix.debug_middleware` path. Only the message changes: the debug tools still fail closed in that configuration.

## [6.0.0] - 2026-10-14
### Security
- pyinstrument is gated like the debug toolbar. With `DEBUG_PYINSTRUMENT`, `autoload()` installs `codenerix.debug_middleware.ProfilerMiddleware`, a subclass of pyinstrument's middleware that only profiles requests accepted by the new `codenerix.debug.show_pyinstrument` (a project `PYINSTRUMENT_SHOW_CALLBACK`, if set, still applies on top). Before, `pyinstrument.middleware.ProfilerMiddleware` was appended to `MIDDLEWARE`, behind the page cache: any client could get a profile with `?profile`, and the profile page was cached and served to everyone asking for that URL, even with a restrictive `PYINSTRUMENT_SHOW_CALLBACK`. The profile page is sent with `Cache-Control: no-store`. Projects that list pyinstrument's own middleware in `MIDDLEWARE` keep it where it is; the gate then applies only if they set `PYINSTRUMENT_SHOW_CALLBACK = "codenerix.debug.show_pyinstrument"`.
- Debug requests (those `codenerix.debug.is_debug_request()` accepts) bypass Django's page cache: they are never served from it and never stored in it, and their responses carry `Cache-Control: no-store`. When it installs a debug tool, `autoload()` replaces `django.middleware.cache.UpdateCacheMiddleware`, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` and `CacheMiddleware` in `MIDDLEWARE` with the `codenerix.debug_middleware` subclasses of the same name, which behave as Django's for every other request. This closes two leaks the 5.3.3 placement left open with the toolbar on: the toolbar's own endpoints (`__debug__/render_panel/` and the like) were cacheable and then served to any client asking for the same URL, and instrumented responses carried `Cache-Control: max-age=600`. Toolbar captures are also always cache misses now.
- The superuser check works for middlewares placed before `SessionMiddleware` and `AuthenticationMiddleware`, where every debug middleware sits since 5.3.3. Without `request.user`, the gate reads the user from the session cookie, read-only: a session whose hash only verifies against `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` is rejected rather than rotated, so the gate never logs the request out. In 5.3.3 the superuser check silently failed closed at that position.

### Changed (breaking)
- One set of settings drives every debug tool (toolbar, debug_panel and pyinstrument), read per request by `codenerix.debug.is_debug_request()`:
  - `DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS` replaces `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_ALLOWED_IPS`. When it is not defined, nobody sees the debug tools: `INTERNAL_IPS` and upstream's default callback no longer apply. `"0.0.0.0"` still means any client (constant renamed to `codenerix.debug.DEBUG_ANY_IP`).
  - `DEBUG_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER` replaces `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER` and defaults to `True` with `DEBUG` on or off (it defaulted to `not DEBUG`). A development setup that should show the tools without logging in must set it to `False`.
  - `autoload(..., DEBUG_PRODUCTION=False)` replaces `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_PRODUCTION`, and now also enables pyinstrument with `DEBUG=False`. The old keyword raises `TypeError`.
  - The removed settings are not read as aliases: if any of them is still defined, system check `codenerix.E001` reports it and every debug tool stays off.
- With `DEBUG=False`, `"0.0.0.0"` in `DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS` requires `DEBUG_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER = True`; without a login, production needs an explicit list of client IPs. Same rule as before, under the new names.
- `autoload()` places pyinstrument's middleware like the toolbar's: at the start of `MIDDLEWARE` or right after `GZipMiddleware`, outside the toolbar (`[profiler, toolbar, ...]`). Projects with `DEBUG=True` and `DEBUG_PYINSTRUMENT=True` see it move from the end of `MIDDLEWARE` to the start, and it now needs `DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS` (and a superuser login, by default) to profile anything.
- The toolbar stands aside on requests carrying pyinstrument's URL argument (`PYINSTRUMENT_URL_ARGUMENT`, `profile` by default) when a profiler middleware is in `MIDDLEWARE`: the profile replaces the response, so the two never run on the same request and the toolbar's cost stays out of the profile. With `PYINSTRUMENT_PROFILE_DIR` (every request profiled to disk), requests without that argument still run the toolbar, and its cost is in those profiles.
- `codenerix.debug.debug_toolbar_access_errors` is now `debug_access_errors`, and the system check function `codenerix.checks.check_debug_toolbar_access` is now `check_debug_access`. The check id stays `codenerix.E001`. It now also reports a profiler middleware placed behind the page cache, stock page cache middlewares left in `MIDDLEWARE` next to a debug tool (for instance when the project adds them after calling `autoload()`), and a toolbar middleware whose `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_CONFIG["SHOW_TOOLBAR_CALLBACK"]` is not `codenerix.debug.show_toolbar` (upstream's callback ignores `DEBUG_ALLOWED_IPS`). The tool concerned fails closed on its own placement errors; a misplaced profiler does not switch the toolbar off, nor the other way round. Like any `Error`, these stop `runserver` and `migrate`.

## [5.3.3] - 2026-10-13
### Security
- `autoload()` inserts the debug toolbar middleware (`DebugToolbarMiddleware`, or `DebugPanelMiddleware` with `DEBUG_PANEL`) at the start of `MIDDLEWARE`, or right after `GZipMiddleware` when present, instead of appending it. Appended, it sat behind Django's page cache (`UpdateCacheMiddleware` ... `FetchFromCacheMiddleware`): responses without cookies were cached with the toolbar output (`djdt-request-id`, `Server-Timing` with SQL timings, or the full toolbar HTML) and served to every client, and cache hits bypassed the toolbar. A toolbar middleware already present in `MIDDLEWARE` keeps its position. Other extra middlewares are still appended.
- `show_toolbar` fails closed, and system check `codenerix.E001` reports it, when a toolbar middleware comes after a page cache middleware in `MIDDLEWARE`, with or without `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_ALLOWED_IPS`. This covers settings that add the page cache after calling `autoload()`, and Django's recommended `UpdateCacheMiddleware` before `GZipMiddleware` order, where the toolbar cannot sit both after GZip and before the cache.

## [5.3.2] - 2026-10-07
### Security
- `OTPAuthMiddleware` only follows the post-login `next` parameter when it points to the current host (`url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme`); anything else redirects to `LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL`.
- `GenList` bounds the client-supplied `pages_to_bring` to the number of existing pages (new helper `codenerix.views.bounded_pages_to_bring`); non-numeric values fall back to 1.
- The LDAP backend no longer forces TLS 1.0 when `AD_SSL` is enabled; ldap3 now negotiates the protocol with Python's default SSL context (TLS 1.2 or later). Directory servers that only speak TLS 1.0 will no longer connect.

### Changed
- `ThrottledAdminEmailHandler` no longer reads the clock at import time to initialise its cleanup timer.

## [5.3.1] - 2026-10-07
### Added
- `OTPAuth` logs every failed attempt (unknown user, wrong password or wrong OTP code) to the new `codenerix.auth.otp` logger as `OTPAuth failure ip=<address>`, with the client address from `REMOTE_ADDR` and nothing else, so it can be watched by fail2ban. Successful logins, OTP-by-email requests with valid credentials and calls without an `authtoken` are not logged. The README documents the `LOGGING` setup and a fail2ban filter and jail.

### Changed
- `file64`, `static64` and `media64` template tags reject paths that resolve outside their base directory (`STATIC_ROOT`, `MEDIA_ROOT` or the given `basepath`) with `SuspiciousFileOperation`. The check is lexical: symlinks inside the base directory keep working, and a leading `/` is still relative to the base, as before.

## [5.3.0] - 2026-10-07
### Added
- `codenerix.debug.show_toolbar`, now the `SHOW_TOOLBAR_CALLBACK` in `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_DEFAULT_CONFIG`. Visibility is driven by the new optional settings `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_ALLOWED_IPS` (list of client IPs; `"0.0.0.0"` means any) and `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER` (defaults to `not DEBUG`). Both are read per request, so they may be defined anywhere in the settings module. When `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_ALLOWED_IPS` is not defined, upstream's default (`DEBUG` and `INTERNAL_IPS`) applies unchanged. The project must assign `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_CONFIG = DEBUG_TOOLBAR_DEFAULT_CONFIG` (or set `SHOW_TOOLBAR_CALLBACK` to `"codenerix.debug.show_toolbar"` itself), and do it under the same condition `autoload()` uses to install the toolbar; otherwise upstream's callback stays in charge and none of this applies.
- `autoload(..., DEBUG_TOOLBAR_PRODUCTION=False)`: opt-in to install the toolbar with `DEBUG=False`. Without it, the toolbar is still only installed when `DEBUG` is true.
- System check `codenerix.E001`: rejects a `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_ALLOWED_IPS` that is not a list/tuple/set (a bare string would degrade into a substring match) and the combination `"0.0.0.0"` + `DEBUG=False` + `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_REQUIRE_SUPERUSER=False`, which would make the toolbar public. `show_toolbar` fails closed on both, since system checks do not run under uWSGI/WSGI.

### Changed
- `autourl()` mounts `__debug__/` when `DEBUG_TOOLBAR` is set and `debug_toolbar` is in `INSTALLED_APPS`, instead of requiring `DEBUG`. It now follows what `autoload()` actually installed.

## [5.2.0] - 2026-09-22
### Added
- `codenerix/tests/test_debug_toolbar.py`: guards against upstream drift. The panel tuple must match django-debug-toolbar's `PANELS_DEFAULTS` verbatim (order included), every dotted path must import against the installed toolbar, and every `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_DEFAULT_CONFIG` key must still be recognised by it.

### Changed
- `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_DEFAULT_PANELS` now mirrors the full upstream default panel set, adding `AlertsPanel` (upstream 4.4.3), `CommunityPanel` (6.1) and `TasksPanel` (7.1). The hardcoded list had silently fallen behind upstream.
- Minimum `django-debug-toolbar` raised from `>=4.4.2` to `>=7.1.0`. Panels are imported by dotted path, so older releases raise `ImportError` on the panels they do not ship.

### Removed
- `INTERCEPT_REDIRECTS` dropped from `DEBUG_TOOLBAR_DEFAULT_CONFIG`. Upstream removed the setting in django-debug-toolbar 2.0 and it had been silently ignored ever since; `DISABLE_PANELS` supersedes it.

## [5.1.3] - 2026-09-18
### Bugfix
- `CodenerixMetaType.__getattr__` now re-raises `AttributeError` for dunder names instead of returning `None`. Returning `None` broke every protocol that probes for an optional special method via `getattr()`: pickle asked for `__setstate__`, got `None` instead of `AttributeError`, then called it, so pickling any model carrying a `CodenerixMeta` failed with `'NoneType' object is not callable`. Non-dunder attributes still fall back to `self.get(attr)`.

## [5.1.2] - 2026-09-10
### Bugfix
- `autoload()` in `debug.py` no longer appends its debug/profiling middlewares (`MessageMiddleware`, `DebugPanelMiddleware`, `DebugToolbarMiddleware`, `snippetscream.ProfileMiddleware`, `pyinstrument`) when they are already present in the project's `MIDDLEWARE`. Previously they were attached unconditionally, duplicating any middleware the programmer had already declared.

## [5.1.1] - 2026-09-10
### Bugfix
- `ThrottledAdminEmailHandler._memory_storage` was only annotated, never assigned, so the attribute did not exist. When the cache backend was unavailable and the handler fell back to in-memory throttling, accessing `self._memory_storage` raised `AttributeError`. It is now assigned as a class-level dict, matching the class-level `_memory_lock`, so the process-wide fallback store actually exists.

## [5.1.0] - 2026-08-17
### Security
- `get_client_ip()` no longer trusts the leftmost `X-Forwarded-For` entry. Nginx's `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for` appends the peer address to whatever the client sent, so the leftmost entry is attacker controlled: any visitor could impersonate an arbitrary IP address. The header is now read from the right, counting the number of trusted hops.
- `GenReCaptchaField.get_remote_ip()` now routes through `get_client_ip()` instead of reimplementing the lookup. It previously handed reCAPTCHA the entire raw `X-Forwarded-For` string, commas included, whenever the header carried more than one entry.

### Feature
- New `CODENERIX_TRUSTED_PROXIES` setting (default `1`) declaring how many trusted reverse proxies sit in front of the application: `1` for a single nginx, `2` for CDN + nginx, `0` or less to distrust proxy headers entirely and use `REMOTE_ADDR` only. `get_client_ip()` also accepts it as a `trusted_proxies` keyword argument, which takes precedence over the setting.
- `get_client_ip()` falls back to `X-Real-IP` when no `X-Forwarded-For` is present. Unlike `X-Forwarded-For`, the proxy overwrites `X-Real-IP` on every request, so it cannot be appended to by the client.
- First test module of the suite: `codenerix/tests/test_helpers.py`, covering the ten header scenarios plus the setting resolution.

### Changed
- **Deployments behind a CDN must set `CODENERIX_TRUSTED_PROXIES`.** With the default of one trusted hop, an application behind CDN + nginx now resolves to the CDN edge address instead of the visitor address. Setting the value to the real number of hops (`2` for CDN + nginx) restores the visitor address. Deployments with a single reverse proxy, or with no proxy headers at all, are unaffected and return exactly the same value as before.

### Maintenance
- Annotated the `defaults` dicts in `fields.py` as `dict[str, Any]`, silencing 66 basedpyright `reportArgumentType` errors caused by the dict being inferred as `dict[str, type[Widget]]` and checked against every keyword parameter of `formfield()`. No behaviour change.

